==================================================================
 WHAT THIS MEASURED
==================================================================

  1. SEASONALITY DOES NOT PRODUCE FALSE ALARMS HERE. IT PRODUCES
     BLINDNESS. Runs A and B agree on every number that matters:
     the same leader, the same z to two decimals, nothing clearing
     either time. One of them has a cell that lost 45% of its
     revenue. That cell's z went from +0.52 to -0.09 -- the
     collapse made it look MORE ordinary, because the seasonal
     spread it is judged against is so wide that a real failure
     lands in the middle of it.

  2. WITHOUT R13 THE HEADLINE IS WORSE THAN THE SILENCE. The net
     test establishes a 50.1% decline at t = -4.49 and shares were
     duly reported: a confident attribution of an ordinary January
     to whichever cell sells the most toys. The signature is in
     the data and costs nothing to look for -- 22 of 24 cells moved
     the same way, which is a one-in-thirty-thousand event if a
     cell is as likely to rise as to fall. A movement common to
     every cell is not explained by any of them.

  3. THE FIX IS THE COMPARISON, NOT THE NULL. Run C is the same
     data, the same library and the same threshold, asked January
     against January: West / Grocery, z -4.91, rank 1, clears.
     Design §7 had declared `versus_last_year` for two months and
     `reasoning.comparisons()` returned one value, so the remedy
     for seasonality could not be spelled. That is fixed here.

  4. AND THE NULL STILL MATTERS, FOR THE OTHER FAULT. Run D plants
     nothing and Southeast / Home clears anyway at z 3.59 -- the
     miscalibration already measured for the t threshold, seen
     live. Runs E and F repeat C and D under siblings_permuted:
     the false one goes away and the true one survives. That is a
     confirmation on a population the permuted null was never
     tuned against.
